# /etc/polkadot-metanode/metanode.env: the variables of the metanode units, the same file on every
# machine (install.sh --config FILE installs it). Each unit passes them to the flags of setup.md's
# commands; `systemctl cat polkadot-<role>` shows which. Restart a role to apply a change, and run
# `polkadot-metanodectl doctor` to check them.
#
# The syntax is systemd's EnvironmentFile=: one KEY=value per line, quotes optional; a # starts a
# comment only at the beginning of a line.
#
# For one machine only, set variables in /etc/polkadot-metanode/polkadot-<role>.env instead, which
# overrides this file for that role: e.g. NODE_NAME, RPC_LISTEN_IP, PUBLIC_IP behind NAT, EXTRA_ARGS.

# The network and the chain of each role. install.sh --network NAME (polkadot, kusama, westend or
# paseo) writes them. A chain is an id built into the binaries, or the absolute path of a chain spec;
# Paseo's specs are downloaded by install.sh.
NETWORK=
RELAY_CHAIN=
ASSET_HUB_CHAIN=
PEOPLE_CHAIN=

# The collators' relay chain RPC endpoints, separated by spaces, in order of preference. Default: the
# validator's safe RPC on this machine. Collators on a machine without the validator list it first
# (its RPC_LISTEN_IP must then be an address they reach). Public endpoints can follow as fallbacks:
# with polkadot-sdk#13525 the collators go back to the first one once it is available again, without
# it they stay on the fallback until it fails. doctor checks that each serves the right relay chain.
# For example, on Paseo:
#RELAY_CHAIN_RPC_URLS=ws://127.0.0.1:9944 wss://paseo-rpc.n.dwellir.com wss://pas-rpc.stakeworld.io
# Empty: every collator runs its own embedded relay chain node instead.
#RELAY_CHAIN_RPC_URLS=ws://127.0.0.1:9944

# The addresses advertised for p2p and for light clients over WebRTC (--public-addr): auto (the
# public source addresses of the default routes, IPv4 and IPv6), none, or the addresses themselves,
# e.g. the public one of a machine behind NAT, which forwards the p2p ports (TCP and UDP).
#PUBLIC_IP=auto

# The safe RPC endpoints: the address they listen on, and how many connections each allows (setup.md:
# 500 on the validator, 200 on the collators; a load test of 25 users exhausted 200). Set the address
# to a private one for collators on other machines, or a WSS proxy elsewhere; an IPv6 address goes in
# brackets, e.g. [::].
#RPC_LISTEN_IP=127.0.0.1
#RPC_MAX_CONNECTIONS=500

# The collators' sync mode: warp (the default), full, fast or fast-unsafe. Warp sync needs the
# parachain registered on the relay chain, so a dev or test parachain needs full.
#SYNC=warp

# The node names in telemetry and logs are "<NODE_NAME>-<role>". Default: the short hostname.
#NODE_NAME=

# Extra flags, split on spaces: EXTRA_ARGS for the node, RELAY_EXTRA_ARGS for a collator's relay
# chain node (after "--"). A flag the unit already sets cannot be repeated; replace the unit's
# ExecStart instead (README.md, "Configure").
#EXTRA_ARGS=
#RELAY_EXTRA_ARGS=

# Bandwidth reservation for the validator (operators/bandwidth.md): the uplink's committed rate, the
# bandwidth your provider guarantees, e.g. 1gbit or 10gbit. When set, install.sh enables
# polkadot-metanode-qos.service, which guarantees the validator VALIDATOR_UPLINK_MBIT of it whatever
# the collators and their light clients send. Needed when the roles share an uplink of 10 Gbit/s or
# less; not supported on Linux 6.19.x and 7.0.0 to 7.0.9.
#UPLINK_RATE=
# The uplink interface. Default: the interface of the default routes, which must be a single one.
#UPLINK_DEVICE=
#VALIDATOR_UPLINK_MBIT=500
